    <div class="hc-col">
      <span class="label">How it works</span>
      <h2>Where each number comes from</h2>
      <p><strong>The WBGT</strong> is estimated with the Australian Bureau of Meteorology model: <span class="hc-num">WBGT = 0.567 × T<sub>air</sub> + 0.393 × e + 3.94</span>, where <span class="hc-num">e</span> is the vapour pressure derived from the dew point. <strong>It carries no solar radiation and no wind term</strong>, so it approximates a shaded value: in direct sun the real WBGT is higher. Treat it as a floor, not a central estimate.</p>
      <p><strong>The penalty</strong> applies the published coefficient of <span class="hc-num">0.3–0.4%</span> per degree of WBGT above <span class="hc-num">15 °C</span>, measured across <span class="hc-num">1,258</span> races and <span class="hc-num">7,867</span> athletes. For mid-pack it multiplies by 2 to 3, from large-marathon data where 300th place lost <span class="hc-num">7.9%</span> while 25th place lost <span class="hc-num">2.6%</span>.</p>
      <p><strong>The acclimation discount</strong> is the softest of the three: it assumes a complete block compresses the penalty by <span class="hc-num">30</span> to <span class="hc-num">50%</span>. That sits inside the published range, but it is an estimate, not a measured coefficient.</p>
      <p class="hc-small">The combination of the three is derived, not measured. No validated WBGT-to-pace table has ever been published. This calculator is the explicit arithmetic of real coefficients — which is different from a number out of nowhere, and different from a guaranteed result.</p>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

